  "data": {
    "variables": [
      "n"
    ],
    "target": "n^2 + n",
    "goal": "even"
  },
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Nothing is assumed about n, so consider every possibility: n is even or n is odd.",
    "Write one case step for each possibility, and finish each case with its own witness."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Case 1: $n = 2k$. Then $n^2 + n = 4k^2 + 2k = 2(2k^2 + k)$, which is even.",
    "Case 2: $n = 2k + 1$. Then $n^2 + n = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 + 2k + 1 = 4k^2 + 6k + 2 = 2(2k^2 + 3k + 1)$, which is even.",
    "Every integer is even or odd, so $n^2 + n$ is always even. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "sundstrom-mr3",
    "section": "3.4 Using Cases in Proofs",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
